{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"dfe2634b8c388877c66c1ab328fa3d19e74a9bd115c0d2d74004e2f6b50fcf5b","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_d7a35454bc7967a4766e5b7418121792cd870e90aef0c78d47cd1e04e1b6a25f","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a technically sound and well-motivated approach to extracting disentangled representations from pretrained GANs, effectively bypassing the hyperparameter tuning issues associated with regularized VAEs. The experimental design is robust, utilizing multiple datasets, baselines, and downstream tasks to validate the claims. The authors demonstrate transparency by reporting variance across seeds and acknowledging specific failure modes, such as the collapse of one model on the MPI3D dataset. The methodological contribution, particularly the DeepSpectral technique, is clearly defined and supported by empirical evidence showing competitive performance with state-of-the-art methods.","paper_id":"VCAXR34cp59","paper_identity_sha256":"27d562570905a8a051f95e0e2bb5b3ec581ea0d711e45f651ce91b409a13335f","prompt_identity_sha256":"533723edbff462901e1538253da86822bf80e8bf1d5972ad7715594b2a837305","raw_response_sha256":"0fdb48d632dfc14f76b68db0f296da9daebd8c33fc25c8c01bd72656f362fd71","request_identity_sha256":"ffef03c4ea0b6784901ef1fe792be0c432a85911d8d54c9ff58b3d33ac008561","reviewer_identity_sha256":"c1cd7c7fdd4a2cbeb45c2f21af982ad0f30bffae8ae148e3eba36479685cf360","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"1d1a7ab72db31e0a7a9f8f29377b39dcede5d1d8e08487b09eb92d60597fc1ba","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-011","SRC-013","SRC-017","SRC-019"],"review_text":"The proposed two-stage procedure is mathematically consistent and well-formulated. The method leverages the latent space of pretrained GANs by identifying orthogonal interpretable directions and subsequently training an encoder to map images to these directions. The formulation of the DeepSpectral (DS) method, which computes singular vectors of the Jacobian of intermediate generator layers, is a clear generalization of the ClosedForm (CF) method and is technically sound. The use of a change of basis to obtain disentangled representations is a straightforward and correct application of linear algebra principles. The paper correctly identifies that the orthogonality constraint is maintained by construction in several methods or can be enforced via QR projection, ensuring the validity of the resulting representation space.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-MET"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-022","SRC-025","SRC-026","SRC-027"],"review_text":"The experimental design is comprehensive, employing four distinct datasets (Cars3D, 3D Shapes, MPI3D, and Isaac3D) to evaluate the proposed methods. The evaluation utilizes established disentanglement metrics (Modularity and MIG) and includes downstream tasks such as abstract reasoning and fairness, which provides a multi-faceted validation of the learned representations. The choice of baselines includes both VAE-based models from prior large-scale studies and GAN-based methods like InfoStyleGAN, ensuring a relevant comparison. The authors also conduct ablation-like studies by varying the depth of the generator layers used in the DeepSpectral method, which helps isolate the contribution of the proposed technique. The inclusion of multiple random seeds for both GAN training and encoder training further strengthens the experimental rigor.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-002","SRC-022","SRC-023","SRC-024"],"review_text":"The paper provides sufficient detail for reproducibility, including the specific datasets used, the architecture of the StyleGAN 2 model, and the hyperparameters for the disentanglement methods. The authors explicitly state that code and models are publicly available, which is a significant strength for reproducibility. The description of the DeepSpectral method includes the specific layers and base points used, and the paper references appendices for additional hyperparameter details. The use of open-source implementations for metrics and downstream tasks (e.g., WReN) further aids in replicating the results. The paper clearly defines the random seed protocol, specifying the number of GANs and encoders trained for each dataset, which is crucial for understanding the variance in the reported results.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-REP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-005","SRC-008","SRC-028","SRC-032"],"review_text":"The paper effectively positions its contributions relative to prior work, comparing the proposed post hoc disentanglement approach with both VAE-based methods and other GAN-based controllable generation techniques. The comparison with VAEs is grounded in the large-scale study by Locatello et al. (2019b), providing a strong baseline for disentanglement metrics. The inclusion of InfoStyleGAN as a direct GAN-based competitor is appropriate, as it represents a state-of-the-art method that combines disentanglement with high-quality generation. The authors also compare their method against other controllable generation techniques like ClosedForm, GANspace, and LatentDiscovery, demonstrating the relative performance of the proposed DeepSpectral method. The paper acknowledges the limitations of some prior methods, such as the hyperparameter sensitivity of VAEs, which highlights the advantage of the proposed approach.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-CMP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-027","SRC-029","SRC-030","SRC-031"],"review_text":"The paper demonstrates statistical rigor by reporting mean and standard deviation for the MIG scores across multiple random seeds. The authors explicitly discuss the variance due to randomness in both the GAN training and encoder training, providing a transparent view of the stability of the results. The observation that the variance for the proposed methods is smaller than for VAEs is a significant finding, supported by the reported standard deviations. The authors also acknowledge a specific failure case where one of the models trained on MPI3D collapsed, which is an honest and important disclosure that affects the interpretation of the results for that dataset. The use of multiple seeds (six GANs and five encoder seeds for most datasets) provides a reasonable basis for statistical inference, although the paper does not perform formal significance tests, which is common in this field.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-STA"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"85e97b70c9d888963000e8e11ff0d99477cc5fd85a494c3fbc7a8f8d961b6790","paper_id":"VCAXR34cp59","paper_ordinal":108,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"0fdb48d632dfc14f76b68db0f296da9daebd8c33fc25c8c01bd72656f362fd71","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"VCAXR34cp59","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"9858d15c2149e0eeb3afba659e45fd1afac8c4a020acf518064f3b754cc8ba05","request_identity_sha256":"ffef03c4ea0b6784901ef1fe792be0c432a85911d8d54c9ff58b3d33ac008561","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v5","usage":{"completion_tokens":1313,"prompt_tokens":18111,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":19424},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"ffef03c4ea0b6784901ef1fe792be0c432a85911d8d54c9ff58b3d33ac008561","role":"QUAL","role_ordinal":0,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}